  ],
  "text": "The Sun delivers more energy to Earth in one hour than humanity uses in a year. So supply was never the problem. Not once. The problem was cost, and that has collapsed. A solar panel costs a fraction of what it did twenty years ago. It is now the cheapest new power almost everywhere. Which moves the whole argument somewhere else. The sun sets. The wind drops. Demand does neither. Peak solar is the middle of the day. Peak demand is the evening. So the fight is no longer about generating it. It is about storing it and moving it. Batteries, pumped water, long distance lines, and the materials for all three. Generation is close to solved. The hard engineering moved downstream. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
